how are most americans feeling about the  future and why are currently employed  americans particularly gen z and  millennials looking for different jobs  this year we track down the answers  there are definite signs americans are  feeling a bit more hopeful about 2022  according to fidelity investment's  latest study more than six in 10  americans feel optimistic about the  future despite the unknown the  prevailing sentiment for 2022 is  cautious optimism and that feeling is  even stronger among women and the  younger generations stacy watson senior  vp of life events planning at fidelity  says 72 percent are confident they'll be  in a better financial position this year  people have become better at setting  goals and attaining them and that's  creating hope but despite the optimism  most people have not forgotten what the  country has been through and are viewing  their financial expectations practically  with concerns about inflation and rising  costs the financial actions people took  at the start of the pandemic out of  necessity like budgeting and building  back emergency savings have become  permanent habits now by choice we will  continue to hear about the great  resignation throughout the year  particularly among the younger  generations according to fidelity's  research half of gen z millennials  currently employed say they are looking  to change jobs this year and here's why  aside from money the reasons for  changing jobs are all values based with  stress levels flexibility  better alignment with personal values  all listed as top motivations  she adds before making any career move  really think through the reason for  leaving your job and talk to your  current manager about what you really  want to get out of it and if they can't  fulfill it look elsewhere
 

